WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.202
Here is an article vector: twenty twenty

00:00:03.222 --> 00:00:07.518
six, fourteen, thirty two, eighty seven.

00:00:07.693 --> 00:00:10.186
Every coordinate is active, so it is

00:00:10.206 --> 00:00:12.583
dense. Would you trust it to match heart

00:00:12.603 --> 00:00:14.850
attack with myocardial infarction?

00:00:17.271 --> 00:00:19.853
They are publication year, pages,

00:00:19.873 --> 00:00:22.288
references and citations.

00:00:22.464 --> 00:00:25.085
The vector records bibliographic facts.

00:00:25.247 --> 00:00:27.972
Being dense tells us how many coordinates

00:00:27.992 --> 00:00:30.704
are active; it does not establish semantic

00:00:30.724 --> 00:00:31.412
matching.

00:00:32.337 --> 00:00:35.230
First, is it a vector: an ordered list of

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.392
numbers? Second, is it sparse or dense:

00:00:39.631 --> 00:00:42.201
relatively few active coordinates, or

00:00:42.221 --> 00:00:43.863
mostly active coordinates?

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.497
Third, was the rule specified explicitly

00:00:47.531 --> 00:00:48.992
or learnt through training?

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:51.609
Fourth, what evidence do the dimensions

00:00:51.629 --> 00:00:52.471
preserve?

00:00:53.037 --> 00:00:54.808
These questions describe different

00:00:54.828 --> 00:00:58.055
properties. BM twenty five is sparse with

00:00:58.075 --> 00:01:00.142
a specified weighting formula.

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:02.823
A conventional dense bi encoder uses a

00:01:02.843 --> 00:01:05.439
learnt mapping into latent features.

00:01:05.601 --> 00:01:07.901
A learnt sparse model provides a third

00:01:07.921 --> 00:01:08.867
combination.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.227
In a lexical representation, we can name a

00:01:12.247 --> 00:01:15.449
coordinate: heart, treatment, or a term

00:01:15.469 --> 00:01:17.020
from the index vocabulary.

00:01:17.183 --> 00:01:19.778
In a dense text embedding, coordinates are

00:01:19.798 --> 00:01:22.704
latent. Their contribution to meaning is

00:01:22.724 --> 00:01:25.178
distributed across the representation and

00:01:25.198 --> 00:01:26.608
shaped by training.

00:01:27.175 --> 00:01:30.379
A value of zero point eight one in latent

00:01:30.399 --> 00:01:33.060
coordinate three does not label that

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.528
coordinate citation.

00:01:34.665 --> 00:01:37.299
Even a trained dense text embedding still

00:01:37.319 --> 00:01:39.786
needs evaluation on the intended retrieval

00:01:39.806 --> 00:01:40.598
task.

00:01:41.517 --> 00:01:43.402
SPLADE offers another route.

00:01:43.577 --> 00:01:45.632
It predicts weights over the output

00:01:45.652 --> 00:01:47.797
vocabulary of a transformer language

00:01:47.817 --> 00:01:50.322
model. In the standard implementation

00:01:50.394 --> 00:01:52.810
those coordinates are WordPiece subword

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:53.686
units.

00:01:54.237 --> 00:01:56.610
Having read the text, the model can assign

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:59.483
weight to terms absent from the input.

00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:02.267
A training penalty encourages almost all

00:02:02.287 --> 00:02:04.690
the remaining weights to be zero.

00:02:04.865 --> 00:02:07.796
The result is learnt and sparse, while its

00:02:07.816 --> 00:02:10.450
active coordinates still carry vocabulary

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:11.313
labels.

00:02:12.199 --> 00:02:14.351
Use the chapter’s schematic heart attack

00:02:14.371 --> 00:02:16.593
example. Imagine a learnt sparse

00:02:16.613 --> 00:02:19.634
representation activates heart and attack,

00:02:19.783 --> 00:02:22.366
but also myocardial and infarction.

00:02:22.541 --> 00:02:24.660
The extra words need not occur in the

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.418
original text. Our displayed weights are

00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:29.905
invented teaching values, not measured

00:02:29.925 --> 00:02:31.258
outputs from SPLADE.

00:02:31.816 --> 00:02:34.271
A query activating myocardial and

00:02:34.291 --> 00:02:36.758
infarction can now share coordinates with

00:02:36.778 --> 00:02:37.995
that representation.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.869
The mechanism can bridge vocabulary,

00:02:40.889 --> 00:02:42.363
conditional on training.

00:02:42.539 --> 00:02:44.799
Whole words make the drawing readable; a

00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:47.184
real model activates its own subword

00:02:47.204 --> 00:02:48.111
entries.

00:02:48.997 --> 00:02:51.435
If nearly every vocabulary term received

00:02:51.455 --> 00:02:54.296
weight, posting lists would grow and more

00:02:54.316 --> 00:02:56.473
work could be needed at search time.

00:02:56.623 --> 00:02:59.309
Sparsity constraints limit the active

00:02:59.329 --> 00:03:00.507
representation.

00:03:00.682 --> 00:03:02.647
The exact cost depends on which terms

00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:05.211
activate, their posting lists and the

00:03:05.231 --> 00:03:06.293
implementation.

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:09.508
A single inverted index can carry exact

00:03:09.528 --> 00:03:12.317
term evidence and learnt expansions.

00:03:12.454 --> 00:03:14.753
Learnt sparse retrieval can therefore be

00:03:14.773 --> 00:03:17.356
an alternative to separate lexical and

00:03:17.376 --> 00:03:18.683
dense retrievers.

00:03:18.845 --> 00:03:21.055
It can also supply one route within a

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:22.188
hybrid system.

00:03:23.092 --> 00:03:25.801
Dot product can combine sparse vocabulary

00:03:25.821 --> 00:03:28.416
weights or dense latent features.

00:03:28.579 --> 00:03:30.827
Cosine can also compare different

00:03:30.847 --> 00:03:32.682
numerical representations.

00:03:32.844 --> 00:03:35.260
Choosing either arithmetic operation does

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.554
not make the underlying representation

00:03:37.574 --> 00:03:40.027
dense, learnt or semantic.

00:03:40.177 --> 00:03:42.631
Representation and comparison remain

00:03:42.651 --> 00:03:44.099
separate decisions.

00:03:44.664 --> 00:03:46.435
And neither operation tells us which

00:03:46.455 --> 00:03:48.071
scored items are returned.

00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:51.577
Admission rules, filters, result limits

00:03:51.597 --> 00:03:53.535
and later stages still matter.

00:03:54.438 --> 00:03:56.762
Our bibliographic vector was dense and

00:03:56.782 --> 00:03:58.965
described explicit facts.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:01.775
BM twenty five was sparse and used a

00:04:01.795 --> 00:04:03.450
specified formula.

00:04:03.612 --> 00:04:06.066
SPLADE was sparse and used a learnt

00:04:06.086 --> 00:04:08.179
mapping that could expand vocabulary.

00:04:08.341 --> 00:04:11.221
The dense bi encoder used a learnt mapping

00:04:11.241 --> 00:04:13.347
with distributed latent coordinates.

00:04:13.908 --> 00:04:17.144
For each, ask what evidence survives, and

00:04:17.164 --> 00:04:19.026
test whether that evidence serves the

00:04:19.046 --> 00:04:22.608
user’s need. Density is a shape, training

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:25.056
describes where the rule came from, and

00:04:25.076 --> 00:04:27.582
semantic matching is a capability to

00:04:27.602 --> 00:04:30.172
investigate. Next we ask what piece of a

00:04:30.192 --> 00:04:32.582
paper those representations actually

00:04:32.602 --> 00:04:33.458
describe.
